---
/**
 * The code of conduct the admin's close-group action has always referred to.
 *
 * Written to be honest about scope: DevRel(ish) indexes gatherings, it doesn't
 * host them. Promising to keep someone safe in a room we're not in would be a
 * lie. What we can do is set the bar for being listed here, and remove groups
 * that don't meet it.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const CONDUCT_EMAIL = import.meta.env.CONDUCT_EMAIL ?? "admin@devrelish.tech";
const updated = "August 2026";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Code of Conduct"
  description="What DevRel(ish) expects of the groups listed here, what organizers commit to, and how to report a problem."
>
  <article class="coc">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <header class="coc-head">
        <h1>Code of Conduct</h1>
        <p class="coc-stamp ident">Last updated {updated}</p>
        <p class="coc-lede">
          DevRel(ish) exists to get people in tech into the same room. That only works
          if the room is one people can actually walk into. This is the bar for being
          listed here.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section class="coc-section">
        <div class="rail"><h2 class="rail-title">The short version</h2></div>
        <p>
          Be someone a newcomer is glad to have met. Assume the person you&rsquo;re
          talking to knows something you don&rsquo;t. When you get it wrong &mdash; and
          everyone does &mdash; listen, fix it, move on.
        </p>
        <p>
          The rest of this page is what that means in practice, and what happens when
          someone doesn&rsquo;t.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="coc-section">
        <div class="rail"><h2 class="rail-title">Who this covers</h2></div>
        <p>
          Everyone at a gathering listed on DevRel(ish), and everyone using this site:
          attendees, speakers, organizers, sponsors, and volunteers. It applies at the
          venue, in the group&rsquo;s online spaces, and in one-to-one contact that
          starts at one of these events.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="coc-section">
        <div class="rail"><h2 class="rail-title">What we expect</h2></div>
        <ul class="coc-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Make room for people who just arrived.</strong> Open your circle.
            Explain the acronym. The person standing alone at their first meetup is the
            whole reason this site exists.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Take people at their word</strong> about their own experience,
            identity, pronouns, and name.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Disagree about the work, not the person.</strong> Strong opinions
            about tools are the point. Contempt for the people holding them isn&rsquo;t.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Ask before you photograph, record, or post</strong> anyone. Not
            everyone can be publicly visible at a tech event, and they don&rsquo;t owe
            you a reason.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Accept &ldquo;no&rdquo; the first time</strong> &mdash; to a
            conversation, a photo, a pitch, a drink, a follow.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="coc-section">
        <div class="rail"><h2 class="rail-title">What isn&rsquo;t allowed</h2></div>
        <ul class="coc-list">
          <li>
            Harassment in any form &mdash; including comments about gender, gender
            identity or expression, sexual orientation, disability, neurotype, physical
            appearance, body size, race, ethnicity, nationality, age, religion, or
            technical background.
          </li>
          <li>Unwelcome sexual attention, advances, or imagery.</li>
          <li>
            Deliberate intimidation, stalking, or following &mdash; at the venue or
            afterwards, in person or online.
          </li>
          <li>
            Photography, recording, or posting about someone after they&rsquo;ve asked
            you not to.
          </li>
          <li>Sustained disruption of a talk, a session, or a conversation.</li>
          <li>
            Publishing someone&rsquo;s private information &mdash; address, employer,
            immigration status, health, or an identity they haven&rsquo;t shared
            themselves.
          </li>
          <li>
            Deliberate misgendering, or repeatedly using a name someone has asked you
            not to use.
          </li>
          <li>
            Advocating for or encouraging any of the above, including &ldquo;as a
            joke&rdquo; or &ldquo;just asking questions.&rdquo;
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="coc-note">
          Being asked to stop is not itself harassment. Neither is being told that
          something you said landed badly. The expectation is that you stop.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="coc-section">
        <div class="rail"><h2 class="rail-title">What organizers commit to</h2></div>
        <p>Listing a group here means agreeing to all of this:</p>
        <ul class="coc-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Your event is open to anyone who fits its stated subject.</strong>
            Groups organized for a specific community &mdash; women in tech,
            underrepresented practitioners, a local language group &mdash; are welcome
            and encouraged; say who the group is for on your group page.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>You&rsquo;ll act on reports</strong> rather than wait for them to
            resolve themselves. You can remove someone from your event. You don&rsquo;t
            need proof beyond reasonable belief, and you don&rsquo;t need to run a trial.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>You&rsquo;ll state accessibility honestly</strong> &mdash; stairs,
            step-free access, seating, loud rooms, whether food covers common
            restrictions. &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo; is a fine answer. Silence
            isn&rsquo;t.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>You won&rsquo;t make alcohol the point.</strong> Serve it if you
            like; make sure someone who isn&rsquo;t drinking still has a reason to be
            there and something to drink.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>You&rsquo;ll be reachable.</strong> Attendees need a way to contact
            you that isn&rsquo;t shouting across a room.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="coc-section" id="reporting">
        <div class="rail"><h2 class="rail-title">Reporting</h2></div>
        <p>
          <strong>If you can, tell the organizer.</strong> They&rsquo;re in the room and
          can act immediately. Every group page has a contact form, and organizers list
          a contact address.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>If the problem is the organizer</strong> &mdash; or you&rsquo;ve
          reported something and nothing happened &mdash; write to
          <a href={`mailto:${CONDUCT_EMAIL}`} class="ident">{CONDUCT_EMAIL}</a>. That
          goes to DevRel(ish), not to the group.
        </p>
        <p>Useful to include, if you have it: what happened, when and where, who was involved, whether anyone else saw it, and what you&rsquo;d like to happen. Any of those can be missing. Report anyway.</p>
        <p class="coc-note">
          We won&rsquo;t share your name with the person you&rsquo;re reporting without
          asking you first. We may have to describe the incident to act on it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="coc-section">
        <div class="rail"><h2 class="rail-title">What we can and can&rsquo;t do</h2></div>
        <p>
          Being straight with you matters more here than sounding reassuring.
          <strong>DevRel(ish) is an index, not a host.</strong> We don&rsquo;t run these
          events, we aren&rsquo;t in the room, and we have no staff at the venue. What we
          can do is decide who gets listed.
        </p>
        <p>On a report we will look into it, and we can:</p>
        <ul class="coc-list">
          <li>Ask the organizer for their account and what they did about it.</li>
          <li>Require a specific change before the group stays listed.</li>
          <li>
            Close the group &mdash; it stops appearing in search, listings, and the
            network feed.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          What we can&rsquo;t do: remove someone from a room we&rsquo;re not in, delete
          records from an account we don&rsquo;t control, or act as an investigator or a
          court. <strong>If you are in danger, contact local emergency services first.</strong>
          A code of conduct is not a substitute for that.
        </p>
        <p class="coc-note">
          Because groups and events are published to the AT&nbsp;Protocol network, other
          apps may have already copied a record. Closing a group here removes it from
          DevRel(ish); it can&rsquo;t reach into anyone else&rsquo;s index. Deleting the
          underlying record is the organizer&rsquo;s to do, on their own account.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="coc-section">
        <div class="rail"><h2 class="rail-title">Attribution</h2></div>
        <p class="coc-note">
          Adapted from the
          <a href="https://www.contributor-covenant.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Contributor Covenant</a>
          and the
          <a href="https://berlincodeofconduct.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Berlin Code of Conduct</a>,
          both widely used and freely adaptable. Errors and additions are ours.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .coc {
    padding-block: var(--space-xl) var(--space-lg);
  }

  .coc-head {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .coc-stamp {
    font-size: var(--text-2xs);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    margin-top: var(--space-3xs);
    max-width: none;
  }

  .coc-lede {
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-variation-settings: var(--display-axes);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    line-height: 1.35;
    max-width: 48ch;
  }

  .coc-section {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .coc-section > p + p,
  .coc-section > ul + p {
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .coc-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .coc-list li {
    padding-left: var(--space-sm);
    padding-block: var(--space-3xs);
    border-left: var(--rule-thin) solid var(--color-rule);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .coc-list li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .coc-list strong {
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .coc-note {
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  .coc-section[id] {
    scroll-margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }

</style>
